## The architectural intent (what the specs now say)

The brain layer is governed by these decisions, all of which live in
`.aide/intent.aide` outcomes (intents #1–#16) plus the agent-readable
surface spec (`.claude/commands/aide/brain.aide` for intent #17).
Read them in the root before drilling into any child.

1. **`brain.aide` lives at `.aide/config/brain.aide`.** The
   `.aide/config/` directory is user-owned: scaffolded once at
   `aide_init` if absent, never overwritten by `aide_init` or
   `aide_upgrade` after that. The directory path itself is the
   ownership signal — install/upgrade tooling reads it, no per-file
   allowlist.

2. **The default scaffolded launcher is `@bitbonsai/mcpvault`.** The
   bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper emits `npx
   @bitbonsai/mcpvault <vault-path>`. Hosts that want a different
   brain edit one file and run `npx aidemd-mcp sync`.

3. **Frontmatter schema = exactly two top-level fields:** `name` (a
   human-readable label the package never branches on) and
   `mcpServerConfig` (`command` + `args` that sync writes verbatim
   into `.mcp.json` under `mcpServers["brain"]`). Final shape:

   ```yaml
   ---
   name: obsidian
   mcpServerConfig:
     command: npx
     args:
       - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
       - "D:/notes/my-vault"
   ---
   ```

4. **Body schema = exactly three named sections:** `## Prose`,
   `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`. The closed-vocabulary parser
   rejects unknown top-level `## ` headings as `malformed-body` and
   rejects missing required sections by name. Nested headings inside
   any section MUST use `### ` or deeper — the walker treats every
   `^## .+$` line as a section-boundary candidate.

5. **Each consumer reads only its owned sections:**
   - `tools/brain` (runtime brain entry-point tool) reads `prose` only,
     returns it byte-identical to callers
   - `provisionBrain` reads `playbookHub` and `researchHub` to scaffold
     the hub artifacts on cold install
   - `cli/sync` and `buildBrainState` read frontmatter only; body
     sections are irrelevant to them

6. **Hub artifact bytes flow from `brain.aide`, never from inline
   TypeScript constants.** `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` and
   `VAULT_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE` are deleted; the bytes that used to
   live in those constants are now in the bundled template's
   `## Playbook hub` body section. The user can edit them like any
   other content in their `brain.aide`.

7. **The framework's brain contract = exactly two required hubs.**
   Coding-playbook hub at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
   research hub at `research/research.md`. Anything else in the brain
   (CLAUDE.md, journal, kanban, identity) is the user's choice, not
   part of the AIDE contract. The retired vault-root CLAUDE.md is
   GONE, not deprecated — its scaffolding step, its template constant,
   and its tests are all deleted.

8. **`provisionBrain` is a 5-step pipeline, in this order:**
   (1) Brain config (brain.aide) → (2) Brain root directories →
   (3) Playbook hub → (4) Research hub → (5) Brain MCP entry. Steps
   3 and 4 source content from the brain.aide planned by step 1 via
   `parseBrainAide` / `parseBrainAideFromString`. Step 1 lives under
   `.aide/config/`; subsequent runs treat it as user-owned and never
   would-overwrite. Hub steps surface `would-skip` (with
   `instructions`) when brain.aide is malformed — no silent empty
   scaffolding.

9. **Removed frontmatter fields:** `connector`, `rootPath`,
   `entryFile`, `tools`. The parser actively rejects these (returns
   `malformed-frontmatter` naming every stale field it found) so users
   migrate cleanly rather than carrying dead fields forward.

10. **No code path branches on `name`.** It's a label for the user
    and the agent, never a dispatcher. Different brains may share
    names without affecting code behavior.

11. **No introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args` for any purpose.**
    Don't extract a vault path, don't infer connector identity, don't
    stat any derived path. Reading args for any purpose other than
    passing them through to `.mcp.json` is the same regression as a
    `rootPath` field in different clothing.

12. **Boot reporter brain status states = four:** `ok`,
    `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`. The prior
    `invalid-path` state is gone with `rootPath` — there is no path
    for the package to validate anymore.

13. **`${...}` interpolation of frontmatter values into
    `mcpServerConfig.args` is supported** for advanced users who want
    to DRY a value across argument positions. The default scaffold
    does NOT exercise this surface (the path is inline, byte-for-byte).
    Substitution lives ONLY in `mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync
    time — NEVER in any body section at agent time.

14. **The MCP server never silently rewrites `.mcp.json`.** Mutation
    of the host's MCP wiring runs through one CLI binary surface
    (`<launcher> sync`). Sync is the only mutation surface; the MCP
    runtime never writes `.mcp.json` itself. The agent may invoke the
    sync CLI on the user's behalf via Bash (see intent #17), but the
    binary is unchanged — only the trigger differs.

15. **Storage-agnostic identifiers in code.** No "vault", "obsidian"
    (other than the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder name and
    legacy-key migration prose), "hub note", "wikilink", or "note"
    (used as a synonym for "file") in TypeScript identifiers,
    comments, or string constants in `provisionBrain/index.ts` and
    sibling code. Methodology guidance about how to navigate the brain
    lives in pipeline prompts, never in code constants.

16. **Strict-failure migration for body schema changes.** A
    pre-widening prose-only `brain.aide` returns `malformed-body`
    naming the missing sections; the parser does not silently default
    them. Users hand-edit their file to add the new sections. No
    `aide_upgrade` carve-out (yet — could be added later if it turns
    out users need it).

17. **Agent-invokes-sync for the wiring loop, not user-invokes-sync.**
    When the brain config flow needs to mutate `.mcp.json` (after a
    cold scaffold, when `.mcp.json` has no brain entry, or when the
    brain entry has drifted from `brain.aide`'s `mcpServerConfig`),
    the agent invokes the sync CLI binary via Bash on the user's
    behalf rather than punting the user to their terminal. The
    launcher is recovered from `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]` —
    the same entry Claude Code used to start the AIDE MCP server in
    the current session, so its existence is provable from the fact
    that the prompt is running. Recovering the launcher this way
    lets the recipe work uniformly for prod (`cmd /c npx
    @aidemd-mcp/server sync`), local-dev (`node /path/to/dist/index.js
    sync`), and any custom binary path. The CLI binary remains the
    only `.mcp.json` mutation surface (intent #14 holds — sync stays
    a CLI verb, never an MCP tool); only the trigger changed from
    "user types it" to "agent runs it on user's behalf via the same
    binary." The user's only remaining manual step is restarting
    Claude Code so the new MCP wiring is picked up at session
    startup. The brain MCP server in the running session was launched
    without the brain entry and cannot be hot-reloaded; restart is
    the one step the agent genuinely cannot do for the user. All
    three broken brain states (`no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`,
    `mcp-drift`) route through this single pattern; the orchestrator
    and the brain config skill share the same delegation contract.

