## Anti-regression invariants the next build phase must hold

These are the hard rules from `.aide/intent.aide` outcomes.undesired
plus the post-cycle additions. Any future build phase must not violate
them, even if a quick path through a ticket looks like it could:

- **No code path branches on `name`.** `name` is metadata. If a future
  consumer wants per-brain behavior, push it back into the user's
  prose — never into a registry.
- **No introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args`.** Don't extract a
  vault path, don't infer connector identity, don't stat any derived
  path.
- **No silent acceptance of deprecated frontmatter fields.**
  `connector` / `rootPath` / `entryFile` / `tools` must be REJECTED
  with a `malformed-frontmatter` reason, not stripped on the floor.
- **No silent acceptance of unknown body section headings.** Any
  top-level `## <heading>` not in `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS` returns
  `malformed-body` naming the unknown heading. The required set is
  exactly three: `## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`.
- **No silent default-filling of missing body sections.** A
  pre-widening prose-only `brain.aide` returns `malformed-body`; users
  hand-edit. No `aide_upgrade` carve-out auto-migrates body schema.
- **No server-side substitution in any body section.** All three body
  sections return verbatim. Substitution lives only in
  `mcpServerConfig.args` at install/sync time.
- **No silent `.mcp.json` mutation.** The MCP server reads `.mcp.json`;
  it never rewrites it. Sync (whether user-invoked at terminal or
  agent-invoked via Bash) is the only mutation surface.
- **No overwrite of files under `.aide/config/` on subsequent
  `aide_init` / `aide_upgrade` runs.** Scaffolded once if absent;
  user-owned forever after.
- **No new frontmatter field whose only consumer is the parser's own
  validation.** Every field must name a real runtime consumer.
- **No inline TypeScript constants for hub-artifact bytes.**
  `PLAYBOOK_HUB_TEMPLATE` and `VAULT_CLAUDE_MD_TEMPLATE` were deleted
  for a reason. Hub bytes flow from brain.aide body sections via
  `parseBrainAide`, full stop.
- **No CLAUDE.md scaffolding step under any name.** "Vault CLAUDE.md",
  "brain CLAUDE.md", "navigation hub" — all rejected. The framework's
  brain contract is exactly the two hubs (playbook + research). Users
  who want a CLAUDE.md add their own.
- **No backend-driver dispatch, no connector registry, no
  matcher-by-shape.** Brain identity is whatever the user wrote in
  their `brain.aide`. Adding a new brain is a user-side edit, never
  a code-level extension.
- **No "vault" / "obsidian" (outside allowed exceptions) /
  "wikilink" / "hub note" / "note" tokens in code identifiers,
  comments, or string constants in install-service modules.** The
  allowed exceptions are: the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper folder
  name (describes content, not a code branch), the `.obsidian`
  directory heuristic comment in `brainRootExists` (an
  honestly-labeled implementation detail), and the legacy `obsidian`
  MCP key in migration branches (the key being migrated away from).
- **No reading another consumer's owned body section.** `tools/brain`
  must not read `playbookHub` or `researchHub`. `provisionBrain` must
  not read `prose`. Cross-section reads violate the per-consumer
  ownership contract.
- **No hardcoded sync invocation in any skill or command prompt.**
  When the brain config flow runs sync via Bash on the user's behalf,
  the launcher MUST be recovered from
  `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]` and never named inline. Literal
  invocations like `npx aidemd-mcp sync`, `cmd /c npx
  @aidemd-mcp/server sync`, or `node dist/index.js sync` outside
  explicitly-marked example blocks are a regression — they break the
  recipe for any install shape (local-dev, custom binary path) other
  than the prod one being hardcoded.
- **No terminal-punt prose on the success path of any broken brain
  state.** `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` all route
  through the brain config skill, which applies the fix on the user's
  behalf via Bash. The orchestrator and the skill must NOT instruct
  the user to "go run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` in your terminal" or
  similar as a manual step on the success path. The user's only
  manual step is restarting Claude Code so the new MCP wiring is
  picked up. The Step 4 failure-handler fallback in the brain config
  skill (when `Bash sync` exits non-zero) is the ONE place terminal-
  punt prose is permitted, because the agent has already attempted
  and failed in-session.
- **No re-call of `aide_info` (or any boot state read) after sync
  completes in the same session.** The brain MCP server in the
  running session was launched without the brain entry; sync mutates
  disk state but cannot hot-reload the live server. `aide_info` would
  report disk state (now `ok`) which would not reflect the live
  server's wiring (still unwired) — false confidence that would lead
  the agent to continue with an operationally broken brain. The
  remediation always halts and routes through restart + new `/aide`
  invocation; never tries to "verify" inside the same session.

